<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry IV of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1553–1610</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Henry III of Navarre, Henry of Navarre</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> from 1572 and the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> king of France from 1589. A Protestant leader in the Wars of Religion, he converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> in 1593 to win <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-nantes">Edict of Nantes</a> in 1598, granting toleration to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a>. He brought the Albret and Foix lands in south-west France to the crown, and was assassinated in 1610.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france.jpg" alt="Henry IV of France" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frans Pourbus the Younger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frans_Pourbus_the_Younger_(Antwerp_1569_-_Paris_1622)_-_Henri_IV,_King_of_France_(1553-1610)_-_RCIN_402972_-_Royal_Collection.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
